## 1. The headline number (brief §B8)

> Minimum GPU KV budget at which policy P retains >= 99% of baseline NIAH accuracy at 32K context,
> and the tokens/sec at that point.

{{> ladder_headline }}

**No approximating rung clears the bar below a full budget.** Rungs 5 to 8 come closest and stop at
{{ ladder.summary.best_retention_approximate.max | pct }} retention at a
{{ ladder.summary.best_retention_budget_approximate | budget }} budget — under the bar by
about a percentage point, on {{ ladder.summary.prompts }} prompts, which is one or two prompts'
worth. The brief asked for this number specifically because it is not gameable, and reporting it as
"near-perfect retention at 75%" would be the gameable version.

**That miss is not resolvable at this prompt count, and [Phase 8](phases/PHASE_8.md) measured how
far from resolvable.** Repeating the sweep at four context lengths puts the query-aware rungs within
one prompt of the bar at *every* context — the closest
{{ phase8.analysis.summary.headline_smallest_abs_margin_prompts | .2f }} prompts — while which side
they land on alternates. So the sentence above should be read as "this study cannot place these
rungs relative to the 99% bar", not as "these rungs fall short of it". The two rungs that *are*
resolvable stay resolvable at every context: the window with a sink misses by 13 to 17 prompts, and
rung 9 clears at the smallest budget swept.

**[Phase 9](phases/PHASE_9.md) placed rung 5 as far as NIAH can.** It reran the two budgets that
straddle the bar on {{ phase9.analysis.summary.prompts | int }} paired prompts at
{{ phase9.analysis.summary.context | int }} tokens, under a decision rule committed before the run:

{{> p9_verdicts }}

At a 50% budget rung 5 **fails**: retention
{{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_50.retention | pct }}, and the whole paired 95% CI
({{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_50.retention_ci95.0 | pct }}–{{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_50.retention_ci95.1 | pct }})
sits below 99%. At 75% it retains
{{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_75.retention | pct }} — the bar itself — with a CI of
{{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_75.retention_ci95.0 | pct }}–{{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_75.retention_ci95.1 | pct }}.
Six times the prompts narrowed that interval without moving it off the line, so the defensible
headline for rung 5 at 75% is the interval, not a pass or a fail. The 99% bar is finer than NIAH can
resolve on this machine for any policy that lands near it.

**KL, beside the headline rather than instead of it.** The 99% bar stays the headline because the
brief defined it before any data existed; replacing it with a metric chosen after Phase 9 would be
choosing a question to fit the answer. What teacher-forced KL adds is resolution. On Phase 10's
{{ kl.summary.windows | int }} paired book windows it orders **every** adjacent pair of budgets
within a rung, at every context
({{ kl.summary.within_resolved | int }} of {{ kl.summary.within_comparisons | int }} comparisons
resolved by an exact sign test over windows, {{ kl.summary.within_resolved_wrong_way | int }} the
wrong way), including the case NIAH could not settle: Quest-style selection at 50% moves the
distribution further from the full cache than at 75% in
{{ kl.summary.quest_50_over_75_windows_above | int }} of {{ kl.summary.windows | int }} windows
(p = {{ kl.summary.quest_50_over_75_sign_test_p | .1g }}; mean KL
{{ kl.summary.quest_50_kl | .1e }} against {{ kl.summary.quest_75_kl | .1e }} nats). Across rungs at
the same budget it resolves {{ kl.summary.across_resolved | int }} of
{{ kl.summary.across_comparisons | int }} pairs. Of the
{{ kl.summary.across_unresolved | int }} it does not,
{{ kl.summary.across_unresolved_rung5_vs_rung8 | int }} are rung 5 against rung 8, which make the same
selection, and the next largest group is the window against H2O-style eviction. KL says which condition is closer to the full cache, reliably; it does not
say whether a difference matters for an answer, which is why it supports the headline and does not
replace it.

**One rung clears it, and it is the one that does not approximate.** Rung 9 computes attention over
the non-resident blocks where they already live — on the CPU — and merges the two partial results
with their log-sum-exp normalizers, which is exact. It meets the target at a
{{ ladder.summary.exact_rung_min_budget_meeting_target | budget }} budget, the tightest the sweep
measured, because being exact makes its accuracy independent of the budget entirely.

{{> ladder_operating_point }}

The cost is {{ ladder.summary.exact_rung_tokens_per_s | .1f }} tokens/s against the full cache's
{{ ladder.best.1.tokens_per_s_at_best | .1f }}, a factor of
{{ ladder.summary.exact_rung_over_full_tokens_per_s | .1f }}, plus host RAM the memory axis does not
show. [Phase 7](phases/PHASE_7.md) measures where that goes.

One number in §3 needs saying out loud rather than leaving in a table. Rung 9's best-scoring budget
is {{ ladder.summary.exact_rung_best_retention_budget | budget }}, where it reads
{{ ladder.summary.exact_rung_best_retention | pct }} of the full cache — *above* the cache it
reproduces. **That is not an improvement and cannot be one.** Rung 9's GPU half runs the bf16 kernel
and its CPU half runs float32, so a prompt sitting on a near-tie can land either way; here one did,
in the flattering direction. The same care was taken with rung 8's apparent gain in Phase 5, and for
the same reason: a study that reports rounding as a win has stopped being a measurement.

So the honest reading of §B8 is two sentences, not one. *Skipping* non-resident KV cannot hold the
last percent of accuracy at any budget this study measured. *Computing* it elsewhere can hold all of
it, at a price in latency and host RAM that no one would pay for a context that still fits in VRAM.

That also stops this being a null study in a second way. The accuracy curve is smooth and the memory
curve is steep, so the interesting question among the approximate rungs turns out not to be "which
policy clears 99%" but "what does each percentage point of accuracy cost in VRAM" — which §2
answers. And the latency result in §4 was not predicted by anyone's arithmetic, including the
brief's.

## 2. The frontier

<picture>
  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="figures/ladder_pareto-dark.png">
  <img alt="NIAH accuracy and decode tokens per second against GPU-resident KV, one curve per policy ladder rung" src="figures/ladder_pareto-light.png">
</picture>

Read the left panel as the study's answer: up and to the left is better. Three shapes matter.

**Rung 5 is a vertical line.** Query-aware selection attends to the top-K blocks but keeps every
block resident, so sweeping its budget moves accuracy and not memory at all. That is not a drawing
error, it is the finding that motivated Phases 4 and 5: rung 5's budget buys accuracy, and only the
CPU tier turns it into a memory budget.

**The cheap baselines are not close.** A sliding window with an attention sink is the strong cheap
baseline the brief insisted on including, and it tops out at
{{ ladder.best.2.best_retention | pct }} retention. LRU block eviction is worse than that
({{ ladder.best.3.best_retention | pct }}), for the reason Phase 2 measured: a block-granular LRU
evicts a block before it can observe a use, so it degenerates to a window without a sink.
Attention-score eviction reaches {{ ladder.best.4.best_retention | pct }}. Query-aware selection is
the only family that gets near the full cache.

**Rung 9 is a horizontal line at the top.** Being exact, its accuracy does not move with the budget,
so it runs flat across the whole memory axis at the full cache's accuracy. That is the shape the
whole ladder was reaching for, and the right panel is where it is paid for: rung 9 sits at the
bottom of it.

**The right panel is where the design premise breaks.** Every tier rung sits low and flat: the
curves barely respond to how much KV is resident. §4.

## 3. Every rung, every budget

The VRAM-slot column matters. Phase 4 ran the tier twice — with slots for exactly the attended set
and for twice it — and only the first frees VRAM at the larger budgets. Rungs 8 and 9 were run only
at the 2× setting, so their memory columns are not directly comparable to rungs 6 and 7's.

